insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-gas-sales-agreement-fixes', 1, 'The Commonwealth model gas sales agreement (2025), Article 12.6, states the buyer''s side of the bargain. What does it oblige the buyer to do in each contract year?', '["Take every unit the seller makes available up to MaxDCQ on each day of the year.", "Pay the domestic base price for the whole ACQ whether or not any gas is delivered.", "Take and pay for, or pay for if not taken, at least the take-or-pay quantity.", "Nominate the DCQ daily, settling any gap on the day at the contract price."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Article 12.6 reads: "In each Contract Year Buyer shall be obligated to take and pay for, or to pay for if not taken, a quantity of Gas at least equal to the Take or Pay Quantity." The obligation is annual and runs to the take-or-pay quantity only. MaxDCQ is a ceiling on a day''s proper nomination, and no clause obliges the buyer to take up to it. The domestic base price is a Nigerian sector price quoted only as reported, and it has nothing to do with the model clause. The obligation is reconciled by contract year, and the clause prices no daily gap at the contract price.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-gas-sales-agreement-fixes', 2, 'Which four of the engine''s nine functions does the Associate tier work with?', '["toEnergy, contractQuantities, dailyBalance and takeOrPay.", "toEnergy, contractQuantities, priceSeries and domesticPrice, for the domestic sector.", "dailyBalance, takeOrPay, gsaCashFlows and energyParitySlope, for money and parity.", "contractQuantities, domesticGasObligation, takeOrPay and priceSeries, for the Act."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Associate question is the quantities and one contract year, so it uses volume to energy (toEnergy), the contract quantities (contractQuantities), the daily balance (dailyBalance) and one take-or-pay year (takeOrPay). Contract prices (priceSeries), the Nigerian domestic prices (domesticPrice) and the Domestic Gas Delivery Obligation (domesticGasObligation) are Professional work; energy parity (energyParitySlope) and the whole contract in money (gsaCashFlows) are Expert work.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-gas-sales-agreement-fixes', 3, 'The engine holds some figures as stated constants and refuses to guess others. Which of these is a stated constant it holds?', '["The take-or-pay percentage, which it sets to the model agreement''s printed figure.", "The make-up period, which it takes from ESMAP''s report.", "The seller shortfall damages rate, which it reads from the Commonwealth model.", "The joules in one International Table Btu, 1055.05585262."]'::jsonb, 3, 'UNITS.BTU_IT_J is 1055.05585262, the International Table Btu from NIST Special Publication 811 (2008 edition), Appendix B, exact by definition. The take-or-pay percentage, the make-up period, the recovery order, the end-of-term rule, every price, the seller shortfall rate and the domestic base price are inputs with no default, and a call without one is refused by name. The model agreement leaves its percentages as [## INSERT] for the parties, so there is no printed figure to take.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-gas-sales-agreement-fixes', 4, 'Which set of terms belongs to the Ekene power plant agreement?', '["Take-or-pay 90 percent of the Adjusted ACQ, with make-up for 5 contract years refunded at the end.", "Take-or-pay 80 percent of the Adjusted ACQ, with make-up for 3 contract years forfeited at the end.", "Take-or-pay 80 percent of the full ACQ, with no make-up and a carry-forward capped at 50 percent.", "Take-or-pay 90 percent of the full ACQ, with make-up for 3 contract years refunded at the end of term."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The power plant fixture states a DCQ of 21000 MMBtu per day, MaxDCQ 110 percent, take-or-pay 80 percent of the Adjusted ACQ, make-up for 3 contract years with the end of term forfeit, and no carry-forward. Take-or-pay 90 percent with make-up for 5 contract years refunded at the end, and a carry-forward capped at 50 percent, are the export feed agreement''s terms. Neither agreement applies its percentage to the full ACQ: the take-or-pay quantity is a percentage of the Adjusted ACQ.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-gas-sales-agreement-fixes', 5, 'The Ekene power plant fixture holds a price of 2.18 US$ per MMBtu in every year from 2027 to 2034. What is that figure?', '["The domestic base price reported for 2026, held flat as a stated planning assumption.", "A price the engine derives each year from the Third Schedule principles.", "The domestic base price the Authority set for every year of the term, read from its circular.", "The Fourth Schedule''s National Reference Price, as the power price."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The fixture''s price note reads that the reported 2026 domestic base price (effective 1 April 2026) is held flat as a planning assumption, and that the Authority re-determines it each year (PIA s.167(1)). The engine derives no domestic base price: it is a required input with no default. The regulator''s circular was not read, so no figure is read from it, and the Authority sets it one year at a time. The National Reference Price is the gas based industries'' US$1 per MMBtu, a different term.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-gas-sales-agreement-fixes', 6, 'Why may this course quote the Commonwealth model gas sales agreement word for word while it teaches the AIPN model agreement by concept only?', '["The AIPN text is older than 2021, so the Petroleum Industry Act replaced its clauses in Nigeria.", "The Commonwealth text was gazetted in Nigeria, so it has the force of law in every domestic contract.", "The Commonwealth text is published under Creative Commons Attribution 4.0; the AIPN text is licensed.", "The AIPN text states no take-or-pay clause, so it has nothing about quantities to quote at all."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The Commonwealth model agreement''s front matter reads "This work is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International.", so it may be quoted with attribution. The AIPN model gas sales agreement is a licensed text, and licensed texts are taught by concept only and never quoted. Neither model agreement is law: a model contract is a template the parties adapt, and nothing in the course says the Act replaced one or that the Commonwealth text was gazetted.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-gas-sales-agreement-fixes', 7, 'How does the course name the Domestic Gas Delivery Obligation Regulations 2022 when it cites them?', '["S.I. No. 206 of 2022, Official Gazette No. 74, Vol. 108, 27 August 2021, read the same day.", "Official Gazette No. 142, Vol. 108, 27 August 2021, as a schedule of the Act.", "S.I. No. 74 of 2022, from the regulator''s page, unnumbered and undated.", "S.I. No. 74 of 2022, Official Gazette No. 206, Vol. 109, 23 November 2022."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The sources table names the Regulations as S.I. No. 74 of 2022, Official Gazette No. 206, Vol. 109, Lagos, 23 November 2022, made and commenced 18 November 2022, and read on 2026-09-26. Official Gazette No. 142, Vol. 108, 27 August 2021 is the Petroleum Industry Act 2021 itself. The copy described as unnumbered and undated is the NUPRC flaring Regulations of 2023, which is why flaring is taught as a concept only.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-gas-sales-agreement-fixes', 8, 'On what date was every text this course cites read?', '["27 August 2021, the date the Act was gazetted, for the statute and its Regulations.", "2026-09-26, for every text in the sources table.", "The date of each text''s own edition, so the Commonwealth model agreement is read as of 2025.", "18 November 2022, when the Regulations commenced, for every Nigerian text in the table."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course names each text with its edition or gazette date AND the date it was read, and every text in the sources table was read on 2026-09-26. The gazette date of the Act (27 August 2021), the commencement of the Regulations (18 November 2022) and the model agreement''s 2025 edition are editions, which the course records beside the read date.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-gas-sales-agreement-fixes', 9, 'A learner types heatingvalue in place of heatingValue in a toEnergy call. What does the engine return?', '["A refusal naming heatingvalue as a key it does not accept, with the list of accepted keys.", "A result computed with a default heating value of 1050 Btu/scf, as held in the power plant fixture.", "A result in MMBtu, because key names are matched without regard to capitals.", "The same energy as before, with a reason line warning about the key."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s own words: "heatingvalue is not an accepted key; the accepted keys at the top level are quantity, quantityUnit, heatingValue, heatingValueUnit, heatingValueBasis, referenceConditions". Every call refuses an input key the function does not read, so a misspelt key never silently drops a term. The engine holds no default heating value (1050 Btu/scf is a term the fixture states), and it does not fold capital letters.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-gas-sales-agreement-fixes', 10, 'Which of these comes back from the engine as a result, with a reason printed beside it?', '["A take-or-pay year with the makeUp object left out, which the engine completes with forfeit.", "A volume stated in bcf, which the engine converts to MMscf and notes in the reason line.", "A day on which the gas taken is above the gas made available, which the engine trims to match.", "A deficiency that carries no make-up right."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A result returned with a reason is a result: a deficiency with no make-up right, a nomination trimmed to MaxDCQ, a day excused by force majeure. The other three are refusals and return no figures at all. The engine''s words for them: "makeUp must be an object { periodYears, order, endOfTerm } (no default); got nothing", "quantityUnit must be one of "scf", "Mscf", "MMscf", "Sm3", "MSm3", "MMSm3"; got "bcf"" and "days[0].taken must be at or below the quantity made available 50; got 60".'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-gas-sales-agreement-fixes', 11, 'Where does a learner of this course run the practicals?', '["In a spreadsheet the course provides, whose formulas copy the engine''s rules line by line for study.", "On paper only, because the engine is kept for grading and no panel calls it during the lessons.", "In the course''s own quantity calculator panel, which calls the same vendored engine the lessons quote.", "In a regulator''s online calculator, which the course links to for every Nigerian quantity."]'::jsonb, 2, 'This is an engine course with no Suite app. Each tier has a calculator panel that calls the same vendored engine: the quantity calculator at Associate, the ledger calculator at Professional and the contract calculator at Expert. The learner types or pastes contract terms and the panel prints what the engine returns, every refusal in the engine''s own words and the reasons beside each figure. No spreadsheet, paper-only route or regulator tool is part of the course.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-gas-sales-agreement-fixes', 12, 'Why does every graded number in this course have exactly one right answer?', '["It is rounded to four decimals under the model agreement''s Article 15.4 before it is graded.", "It is a return value of the engine on fixed inputs, and nothing in the engine samples or searches.", "The grader accepts whatever figure a learner''s own spreadsheet returns, within a stated band.", "Each graded figure is copied from a published textbook example, which fixes the answer."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A capstone field, a question key and a panel figure are each computed by an engine function on contract terms written down in advance. Nothing in the engine samples or searches, so the same terms give the same number on any machine. Graded figures are quoted to six decimals as the panel prints them; the Article 15.4 four-decimal rule belongs to contract prices, which are Professional work.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-gas-sales-agreement-fixes', 13, 'Gas sold as LPG or CNG, and turning flared gas into value, come up while reading a gas sales agreement. Where does this course send them?', '["To the gas value course, in one sentence, without re-teaching them.", "To the Petroleum Industry Act course, which owns every use of gas in Nigeria and its fiscal terms.", "To this course''s Expert tier, which prices LPG and CNG beside the oil-indexed export feed.", "To the cash flow course, which values each gas product by its NPV."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The scope seams are recorded: flare to value, LPG and CNG belong to the gas value course; the cash flow ledger, discounting and NPV as a subject belong to the cash flow course; the Nigerian fiscal system belongs to the Petroleum Industry Act course. This course names each seam in one sentence and stays with the contract.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-gas-sales-agreement-fixes', 14, 'What does this course print as the fine for flaring gas in Nigeria?', '["A fine per unit of gas flared, which section 104 of the Act prints in its own text.", "A rate per thousand standard cubic feet read from the NUPRC Regulations of 2023.", "The domestic base price per MMBtu flared, as the Act ties the fine to that price.", "No rate: flaring is taught as a concept only."]'::jsonb, 3, 'PIA s.104(1) makes flaring outside the listed exceptions an offence "liable to a fine as prescribed by the Commission in regulations under this Act", so the Act prints no rate, and the NUPRC Regulations copy read is unnumbered and undated. No flare rate appears anywhere in this course, and no text the course read ties a flaring fine to the domestic base price.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-gas-sales-agreement-fixes', 15, 'The course legislates its vocabulary. How must it write about a gap between gas wanted and gas that came?', '["As a shortfall, since the model agreement''s Shortfall Quantity covers both sides of the delivery point.", "As a seller shortfall or a buyer shortfall, with the side always named.", "As a deficiency, whichever side caused it, measured daily.", "As make-up, whenever the buyer may take the gas later."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The vocabulary rule: "shortfall" is always qualified. A seller shortfall is gas the seller did not make available against a properly nominated quantity; a buyer shortfall is the adjusted DCQ the buyer did not take. A deficiency is the take-or-pay quantity less the quantity counted over a year, and make-up is gas paid for in a deficiency year and taken later. The model agreement''s Shortfall Quantity is the seller''s side only.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-volume-to-energy', 1, 'The Ekene power plant states 20 MMscf per day at 1050 Btu/scf gross. What energy does the engine return for that volume?', '["22156.172905 MMBtu, once the Btu constant is applied to the product.", "21000.000000 MMBtu, on the gross basis the contract states.", "21000.000000 GJ, because the imperial route returns gigajoules first.", "63000.000000 MMBtu for the stated day."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The imperial rule, in the engine''s words: MMBtu = volume in scf x heating value in Btu/scf / 1,000,000. Twenty million scf at 1050 Btu/scf is 21000.000000 MMBtu, the power plant''s DCQ. 22156.172905 is the same energy in GJ, where the constant does apply. The route returns MMBtu as the plain product, and 63000.000000 MMBtu is the export feed''s DCQ from 60 MMscf.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-volume-to-energy', 2, 'The power plant''s 21000.000000 MMBtu is also returned as 22156.172905 GJ. What produces the GJ figure?', '["A metric heating value the engine looks up.", "The MMBtu figure times the thermochemical Btu, a slightly different joule value.", "A net heating value applied to gigajoules.", "The MMBtu figure times 1055.05585262 MJ per MMBtu."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine''s units line reads: 1 MMBtu = 1055.05585262 MJ (International Table Btu, NIST Special Publication 811 (2008 edition), Appendix B). 21000.000000 MMBtu at that exact constant is 22156.172905 GJ. The engine looks up no heating value; the contract states it. It holds the exact defining value, and it changes no basis: the power plant''s figure stays gross in both units.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-volume-to-energy', 3, 'One MMSm3 of gas is stated at 39 MJ/Sm3 gross. What energy in MMBtu does the engine return?', '["36964.867692 MMBtu.", "39000.000000 MMBtu, the plain product of the volume and heating value.", "33268.380923 MMBtu, once the engine reduces the gross value to a net one.", "37080.400058 MMBtu, after it converts the cubic metres into cubic feet."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The metric rule, in the engine''s words: MMBtu = volume in Sm3 x heating value in MJ/Sm3 / 1055.05585262 (MJ per MMBtu). One million Sm3 at 39 MJ/Sm3 is 39000.000000 GJ, and divided through that is 36964.867692 MMBtu. 39000.000000 is the GJ figure. 33268.380923 is the net case at 35.100000 MJ/Sm3; the engine never converts one basis to the other. 37080.400058 is the mixed pair at 1050 Btu/scf.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-volume-to-energy', 4, 'On the metric route, which of the engine''s two figures is the plain product of volume and heating value, with no constant applied?', '["The MMBtu figure: 36964.867692 for the same gas.", "Neither, since both tiles divide by the constant.", "The GJ figure: 39000.000000 for one MMSm3 at 39 MJ/Sm3.", "Both of them, because the metric route carries no constant in any direction."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Volume in Sm3 times MJ/Sm3 gives megajoules, so the GJ tile is the plain product, 39000.000000. The MMBtu tile divides that by 1055.05585262 MJ per MMBtu to give 36964.867692. On the imperial route it is the other way round: the MMBtu figure is the plain product and the GJ figure needs the constant.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-volume-to-energy', 5, 'The net case, one MMSm3 at 35.100000 MJ/Sm3, is entered with heatingValueBasis set to gross. What does the engine return?', '["33268.380923 MMBtu, labelled gross.", "36964.867692 MMBtu, because the engine restores the gross heating value first.", "A refusal, since 35.100000 MJ/Sm3 sits below the gross figure it expects.", "33268.380923 MMBtu, labelled net, because the engine detects the lower value."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine does the same arithmetic on a gross and a net heating value and carries the stated basis through. The net case''s numbers stated as gross return 33268.380923 MMBtu, labelled gross: the label changes and the arithmetic does not. The engine cannot tell a net figure from a gross one, holds no expected value to refuse against, and never converts between them, so the discipline sits with whoever enters the term.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-volume-to-energy', 6, 'A learner sets heatingValueBasis to "higher" because the contract says higher heating value. What happens?', '["It reads higher as gross and returns a gross result.", "The engine refuses the call and names heatingValueBasis.", "A result labelled higher, echoing the word.", "It reads higher as net, its fallback basis."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine''s own words: heatingValueBasis must be one of "gross", "net"; got "higher". Gross and higher heating value mean the same thing, but the input takes the contract word gross or net only, and nothing else is guessed or echoed. The engine''s wording for gross is "gross (higher) heating value, as the contract states".'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-volume-to-energy', 7, 'What does the engine do with the referenceConditions string of a toEnergy call?', '["Converts the volume to 15 C and 101.325 kPa.", "Corrects the heating value for temperature and pressure from the string''s figures.", "Checks it against a list of standard conditions and refuses any it does not know.", "Echoes it on the result, refusing it only when it is blank."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The input must be a non-empty string; the engine''s words for a blank one are "referenceConditions must be a non-empty string; got "  "". It does not interpret the words: it echoes them back so the conditions travel with the figure. The power plant states 60 F and 14.696 psia (synthetic statement) and the golden metric cases 15 C and 101.325 kPa, and no conversion between the two is made.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-volume-to-energy', 8, 'Metric volume, imperial heating value: one MMSm3 at 1050 Btu/scf gross. Which MMBtu figure comes back?', '["39121.893098 MMBtu, the product of the volume and the heating value as stated.", "36964.867692 MMBtu, after the heating value is restated as 39 MJ/Sm3 by the engine.", "37080.400058 MMBtu, the volume converted to scf by the exact factor.", "A refusal, as Sm3 cannot pair with Btu/scf."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine''s rule for this pair: MMBtu = (volume in Sm3 / 0.028316846592 ft3 per Sm3) x heating value in Btu/scf / 1,000,000, which gives 37080.400058 MMBtu and 39121.893098 GJ. So 39121.893098 is the GJ figure. 36964.867692 is the all-metric case at 39 MJ/Sm3, which the engine never substitutes. A mixed pair is accepted, with one set of reference conditions assumed.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-volume-to-energy', 9, 'A contract states its volume at 60 F and 14.696 psia and its heating value at 15 C and 101.325 kPa. What does the engine''s mixed-pair route do about the difference?', '["It corrects the volume from one set of conditions to the other before it multiplies.", "Nothing: it applies only the geometric factor and assumes one set of conditions.", "It refuses the call and names referenceConditions as the field in conflict.", "An average of the two sets of conditions, stated in the reason line."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A mixed pair converts the volume by the exact geometric factor (a cubic foot is 0.028316846592 cubic metres) and assumes one set of reference conditions for both numbers, which the caller states and the engine echoes back. A contract that states its volume and heating value at different conditions needs a conversion the engine does not make; the engine neither corrects, refuses nor averages.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-volume-to-energy', 10, 'Which of these toEnergy inputs does the engine refuse?', '["A quantity of -1.", "A quantity of 0 MMSm3 at 39 MJ/Sm3 gross, since zero gas cannot be sold.", "A heating value stated on the net basis for a volume in standard cubic metres.", "A volume stated in MSm3, one of the metric units, with a heating value in MJ/Sm3."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s own words: quantity must be a finite number at or above 0; got -1. A quantity of zero is a result, 0.000000 MMBtu and 0.000000 GJ, with the rule printed as usual. Net is one of the two bases the engine accepts, and MSm3 is one of its six volume units.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-volume-to-energy', 11, 'A learner enters a heating value of 0 in the volume-to-energy view. What comes back?', '["An energy of 0.000000 MMBtu, as a zero quantity gives.", "A result at the fixture''s 1050 Btu/scf.", "A result of 0.000000 GJ and no MMBtu.", "A refusal: heatingValue must be a finite number above 0; got 0."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The heating value must be above zero, and the message is the engine''s own words. A zero QUANTITY is a result of zero, but a zero heating value is refused. The engine has no default heating value to fall back on: 1050 Btu/scf is a term the Ekene fixtures state.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-volume-to-energy', 12, 'Where does the engine''s 1055.05585262 MJ per MMBtu come from?', '["A rounding the Commonwealth model gas sales agreement prints in its definitions.", "The EIA''s energy conversion calculator.", "The International Table Btu, NIST SP 811 (2008 edition), Appendix B.", "An average of the Ekene heating values."]'::jsonb, 2, 'UNITS.BTU_IT_J is 1055.05585262 joules in one International Table Btu, NIST Special Publication 811 (2008 edition), Appendix B, exact by definition, so a MMBtu is 1055.05585262 MJ. The EIA calculator is read for a barrel of crude oil''s heat content, later-tier material, and no heating value enters the constant.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-volume-to-energy', 13, 'What does the engine return for 1000 Mscf at 1000 Btu/scf gross?', '["1055.055853 MMBtu and 1000.000000 GJ, the two tiles as the metric route prints them.", "1000.000000 MMBtu and 1055.055853 GJ.", "1000.000000 MMBtu and 1000.000000 GJ, because a thousand Mscf needs no constant.", "1055.055853 MMBtu and 1055.055853 GJ, with the constant applied to both tiles."]'::jsonb, 1, 'On the imperial route MMBtu is the plain product: one million scf at 1000 Btu/scf, divided by one million, is exactly 1000.000000 MMBtu. The GJ tile applies the exact constant, 1055.05585262 MJ per MMBtu, to give 1055.055853. The first two distractors swap or drop the constant.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-volume-to-energy', 14, 'Why does a gas sales agreement count its quantities in energy?', '["The buyer is buying heat, and gas from different fields carries different heat per cubic foot.", "Pipeline meters measure energy directly, so no conversion from volume is ever needed.", "The Petroleum Industry Act 2021 forbids a contract quantity stated in cubic feet.", "Energy units remove the need to state a heating value or a basis in the contract."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A buyer burns gas for heat, and two cubic feet from different fields can carry different amounts of it. Meters measure volume, so the contract converts every volume to energy with a stated heating value and basis before it counts anything. No text the course reads bans a volume unit; the contract still states its heating value, gross or net.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-volume-to-energy', 15, 'A seller''s meter records its volume in bcf. What must happen before the engine will read it?', '["Nothing, since the engine converts bcf to MMscf on its own and notes it.", "It is entered in GJ, which the engine treats as base.", "The heating value is restated per bcf, which lets the engine keep the unit.", "It is restated in one of the six units the engine accepts."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine''s own words: quantityUnit must be one of "scf", "Mscf", "MMscf", "Sm3", "MSm3", "MMSm3"; got "bcf". A unit outside the six is refused by name, and nothing is converted silently. The engine takes a volume and returns MMBtu and GJ; it takes no energy as input.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-contract-quantities', 1, 'At the power plant''s DCQ of 21000 MMBtu, what ACQ does the engine return for calendar year 2027?', '["7686000.000000 MMBtu, counting 366 days in 2027 as the engine does for a year that follows a leap year.", "6132000.000000 MMBtu, the take-or-pay share of the year.", "23100.000000 MMBtu for each day, since the ACQ sums MaxDCQ.", "7665000.000000 MMBtu, 365 days at the DCQ."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The rule, in the engine''s basis: ACQ = DCQ x days in the contract year. The engine''s day count reads "calendar year 2027: 365 days (not a leap year)", so 21000 x 365 is 7665000.000000. 7686000.000000 is 2028, a leap year of 366 days. 6132000.000000 is 80 percent of the ACQ, the take-or-pay quantity on the full ACQ. MaxDCQ, 23100.000000, is a daily ceiling and adds nothing to the ACQ.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-contract-quantities', 2, 'The power plant''s ACQ is 7686000.000000 in 2028 and 7665000.000000 in 2027. What accounts for the difference?', '["The DCQ rises in 2028 with the plant''s demand.", "2028 is a leap year of 366 days, one more DCQ than 2027.", "The engine adds back 2027''s force majeure when it counts 2028.", "A period rule: the engine counts 2028 with its end date included, one day more."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine''s day count reads "calendar year 2028: 366 days (a leap year)". At the same DCQ of 21000 the extra day adds exactly one DCQ, 21000, to the ACQ. The fixture''s DCQ is 21000 in every year, force majeure reduces the Adjusted ACQ and never the ACQ, and a calendar year is a calendar year: a period''s end-date rule applies only when a period is stated.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-contract-quantities', 3, 'Calendar year 2100 is run at a DCQ of 1000. Which ACQ comes back?', '["365000.000000, because 2100 is no leap year under the Gregorian rule.", "366000.000000, as 2100 divides by four.", "366000.000000, like the year 2000.", "A refusal, as 2100 lies past MAX_YEARS."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s day count reads "calendar year 2100: 365 days (not a leap year)". A century year is a leap year only when it divides by four hundred: 2000 is one (366000.000000 at the same DCQ) and 2100 is not. DEFAULTS.MAX_YEARS, 100, caps the number of contract years one take-or-pay call accepts; it has nothing to do with a calendar year''s date.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-contract-quantities', 4, 'A first contract year is stated as a period from 2027-07-01 to 2028-01-01 at the export feed''s DCQ of 63000. What does the engine count?', '["A refusal, because a contract year stated as a period must run a whole twelve months.", "The days to 2028-01-01 inclusive, one day more than the engine''s own count.", "184 days and an ACQ of 11592000.000000.", "365 days, because a period is always rounded up to the full calendar year."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine''s own wording: "2027-07-01 up to 2028-01-01 (end date excluded, as a contract year that finishes on the following 1 January): 184 days". 63000 x 184 is 11592000.000000. A period may be any length with its end after its start; the end date is excluded, and nothing is rounded to a calendar year.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-contract-quantities', 5, 'Fifty thousand MMBtu a day over a stated 350 days: which ACQ does the engine return?', '["A result for 365 days, as every contract year is read as a calendar year.", "A refusal, since a contract year must hold 365 or 366 days to be counted by the engine.", "17500000.000000, for 350 days as stated.", "14875000.000000, a share of it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A stated number of days is one of the three ways the engine takes a day count; its wording is "350 days as stated", and 50000 x 350 is 17500000.000000. The engine never substitutes a calendar year for a stated count. 14875000.000000 is that case''s take-or-pay quantity on the full ACQ, a different figure.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-contract-quantities', 6, 'What MaxDCQ and swing factor does the engine return for the power plant''s 110 percent?', '["A MaxDCQ of 23100.000000 and a swing factor of 1.100000.", "A MaxDCQ of 21000.000000 and a swing factor of 1.375000.", "A MaxDCQ of 66150.000000 and a swing factor of 1.050000.", "A swing factor of 1.100000 and no MaxDCQ."]'::jsonb, 0, 'MaxDCQ = maxDcqPct % of DCQ, so 110 percent of 21000 is 23100.000000, and the swing factor is MaxDCQ / DCQ, 1.100000. 1.375000 is the power plant''s EFFECTIVE swing, the swing factor over the take-or-pay fraction. 66150.000000 and 1.050000 are the export feed''s 105 percent of 63000. A MaxDCQ percentage the engine accepts returns a MaxDCQ beside the swing factor.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-contract-quantities', 7, 'The power plant pairs a MaxDCQ of 110 percent with take-or-pay at 80 percent. Which effective swing results?', '["1.100000, the swing factor itself, since effective swing is the swing factor.", "The take-or-pay fraction itself, the level the swing is measured against.", "1.666667, the effective swing of every contract with a swing factor of this size.", "1.375000."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine''s rule: effective swing = swing factor / take-or-pay fraction, so 1.100000 / 0.8 is 1.375000. HMRC OT05402 describes it as "dividing the swing factor by the take or pay level". 1.100000 is the swing factor before the division. 1.666667 belongs to HMRC''s own example of 150 over 90, and the fraction alone is no measure of swing.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-contract-quantities', 8, 'HMRC''s Oil Taxation Manual OT05402 works 150 over 90 and prints 1.66. How does this course quote that effective swing?', '["As 1.66, because the manual is the source and a printed text outranks a computed one.", "As the engine''s 1.666667, naming 1.66 as the figure the manual prints.", "As 1.500000, HMRC''s swing factor, which the course quotes in place of a ratio.", "As 1.660000, the engine''s figure, which matches the manual."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine returns 1.666667. The golden file''s rule says the manual prints the quotient cut after two decimals; that cut is 1.660000, derived, and not the engine''s figure. When a text prints a figure the engine computes more exactly, the course quotes the engine''s and names the text''s printed one as the text''s. The swing factor, 1.500000, is the numerator of the division and no effective swing.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-contract-quantities', 9, 'A learner enters maxDcqPct 90. What does the engine do?', '["Refuses: maxDcqPct must be a number at or above 100 when given.", "Accepts it, giving a MaxDCQ below the DCQ and a swing factor under one.", "Treats it as 90 percent of the take-or-pay quantity instead.", "Clamps it to 100 and returns a swing factor of exactly one."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s own words: "maxDcqPct must be a number at or above 100 when given; got 90". A ceiling below the ordinary daily quantity would make every ordinary nomination improper. Exactly 100 is accepted and means no swing, but the engine does not quietly raise a figure to reach it.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-contract-quantities', 10, 'When does the engine refuse a take-or-pay percentage of 0 in a contractQuantities call?', '["Always, in any call at all.", "Only for a calendar year count.", "Never; 0 gives a quantity of 0.", "When a maxDcqPct is stated beside it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine''s own words: "topPct must be above 0 when maxDcqPct is stated (the effective swing divides by it); got 0". Without a MaxDCQ a 0 is accepted: the golden case returns a take-or-pay quantity of 0.000000 and no effective swing. The day count has nothing to do with it.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-contract-quantities', 11, 'A learner adds "days": 365 to a contractQuantities box that already states a year. What does the engine return?', '["A result for 365 days, the stated count taking priority over the year.", "A refusal: days must be the only day count stated; got days and year.", "A result for the calendar year, and the days entry is dropped.", "Both counts combined, with a reason naming the conflict."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A day count is a stated term, and the engine takes exactly one: a stated number of days, a calendar year or a period. Two at once are refused with the message quoted, the engine''s own words, and none at all is refused with "days must be stated, or replaced by year or by period; got nothing". Nothing is chosen between them or added.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-contract-quantities', 12, 'In the contract quantities view, the power plant''s 2027 shows a take-or-pay quantity of 6132000.000000. What is that figure?', '["The quantity the buyer owes for 2027, after force majeure and seller shortfall.", "The Adjusted ACQ of 2027 that the take-or-pay year view prints.", "80 percent of the full ACQ, before any of the year''s reductions.", "What the buyer took in 2027, as the fixture records it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'contractQuantities applies topPct to the ACQ "before any adjustment", so 80 percent of 7665000.000000 is 6132000.000000, a planning figure. The quantity the buyer owes is a percentage of the Adjusted ACQ, which the one take-or-pay year view computes after the year''s reductions. The fixture''s 2027 take is 7245000.000000.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-contract-quantities', 13, 'On 2027-01-25 the power plant''s buyer nominated 24150 against a MaxDCQ of 23100. What is the properly nominated quantity?', '["23100.000000, with 1050 not properly nominated.", "24150.000000, the whole nomination.", "21000.000000, the DCQ.", "22050.000000, which is 24150 less the 2100 of over-take the day records."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s rule: PNQ = min(nominated, MaxDCQ). Its reason, verbatim: "2027-01-25: nominated 24150 is above the MaxDCQ 23100; 1050 is not properly nominated". The seller owes nothing for the 1050 above the ceiling. The DCQ is no cap on a nomination; MaxDCQ is.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-contract-quantities', 14, 'A contract states no MaxDCQ. On a stated day the buyer nominates, is offered and takes 180 against a DCQ of 100. What does the engine report?', '["A seller shortfall of 80, since the nomination runs above the DCQ.", "A refusal, as a nomination may never exceed the DCQ.", "No reason at all, because nothing is owed either way.", "Trimmed to 100, with 80 not properly nominated."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Without a MaxDCQ there is no ceiling, and every nomination is properly nominated: the golden day daily-no-maxdcq returns a properly nominated quantity of 180.000000 and an empty list of reasons, since nothing is owed either way. The 80 above the DCQ is over-take, taken and paid for, and neither side owes anything for it.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-contract-quantities', 15, 'Four contractQuantities calls are drafted, and only one comes back refused. Which?', '["Calendar year 2000 at a DCQ of 1000, which the engine counts as 366 days.", "A maxDcqPct of exactly 100, which states no swing at all.", "A topPct of 0 with no MaxDCQ stated, and a DCQ of 50000.", "Days stated as 365.5."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine''s own words: "days must be an integer at or above 1; got 365.5". Year 2000 is a leap year and returns 366000.000000 at a DCQ of 1000; a maxDcqPct of 100 is accepted and means MaxDCQ equals the DCQ; a topPct of 0 without a MaxDCQ is accepted and gives a take-or-pay quantity of 0.000000.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-daily-balance', 1, 'Over the 31 days of January 2027, what total buyer shortfall does the engine return for the Ekene power plant?', '["31270.000000, summed over the days on which one arose.", "24740.000000, the month''s buyer shortfall once the over-take of 6530.000000 is netted off it.", "6300.000000, the gap on 2027-01-20.", "8400.000000, from the buyer-caused day."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The January 2027 totals print a buyer shortfall of 31270.000000 and an over-take of 6530.000000, each summed separately. 24740.000000 is the difference between the two, one side of the reconciliation identity and no total the engine labels buyer shortfall. 6300.000000 is the month''s seller shortfall, all of it on 2027-01-20, and 8400.000000 is the buyer shortfall of the one buyer-caused day, 2027-01-30.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-daily-balance', 2, 'The engine''s identity for January 2027 sets the sum of buyer shortfall less over-take against the Adjusted ACQ for the days less taken. What does it find?', '["31270.000000 on one side and 24740.000000 on the other, the gap being the month''s over-take.", "The two sides agree to six decimals, which the course reads as equal whatever the engine holds.", "Both sides equal 24740.000000, checked exactly.", "Both are 592200.000000."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The identity, in the engine''s basis: sum of buyer shortfall - sum of over-take = Adjusted ACQ - taken. Buyer shortfall less over-take is 31270.000000 less 6530.000000; the Adjusted ACQ for the days less taken is 592200.000000 less 567460.000000. Both are 24740.000000, and the course checks them equal exactly. Agreement at six decimals alone would prove nothing: printed alike is not equal.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-daily-balance', 3, 'On 2027-01-20 the power plant''s buyer properly nominated 22050 and the seller made 15750 available, with no tolerance, force majeure or maintenance. What seller shortfall does the engine return?', '["0.000000, because the buyer took every unit of the gas the seller made available.", "1050.000000, the part of the day''s take that ran above the adjusted DCQ.", "22050.000000, the whole of the quantity properly nominated for the day.", "6300.000000."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine''s reason, verbatim: "2027-01-20: the seller made 15750 available against a properly nominated 22050: seller shortfall 6300". The shortfall is the properly nominated quantity less what was made available. Taking all that was offered clears the buyer''s side, and 1050.000000 is that day''s over-take.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-daily-balance', 4, 'The 6300 seller shortfall of 2027-01-20 changes the buyer''s yardstick for that day. To what?', '["21000.000000, the DCQ.", "14700.000000, the DCQ of 21000 less the day''s seller shortfall.", "15750.000000, the gas made available.", "22050.000000, the nomination."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine''s rule: adjusted DCQ = DCQ - force majeure - maintenance - seller shortfall. With no force majeure or maintenance that day, 21000 less 6300 is 14700.000000. The buyer took the 15750.000000 made available, so it has no buyer shortfall and the 1050.000000 above the adjusted DCQ is over-take. Neither the gas offered nor the nomination is the yardstick.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-daily-balance', 5, 'A golden day states a DCQ of 100, a delivery tolerance of 5, a properly nominated 100, and 95 made available and taken. What does the engine return?', '["The tolerance covers the gap; the buyer''s side is 5.000000 short.", "A seller shortfall of 5.000000.", "Both sides short by 5.000000, the same gap counted once as a seller shortfall and once as a buyer shortfall.", "A seller shortfall of 1.000000 and an adjusted DCQ of 99.000000, the tolerance taken first."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The seller shortfall is (PNQ - tolerance) - available: (100 - 5) - 95 is 0, so the tolerance covers the gap. The adjusted DCQ stays 100.000000, and taken 95 leaves a buyer shortfall of 5.000000; the engine''s reason: "2027-03-01: taken 95 is below the adjusted DCQ 100: buyer shortfall 5". A seller shortfall of 1.000000 with an adjusted DCQ of 99.000000 is the day with 94 made available.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-daily-balance', 6, 'Move the seller one unit past its tolerance of 5: 94 offered and taken against 100 properly nominated. Which adjusted DCQ comes back?', '["94.000000, the gas made available and taken.", "95.000000, the DCQ less the tolerance.", "100.000000, the DCQ, left whole because a delivery tolerance day never reduces the buyer''s yardstick.", "99.000000, the DCQ less the seller shortfall of 1."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine''s reasons, verbatim: "2027-03-01: the seller made 94 available against a properly nominated 100 less the tolerance 5: seller shortfall 1" and "2027-03-01: taken 94 is below the adjusted DCQ 99: buyer shortfall 5". The seller shortfall of 1 comes off the DCQ, so the adjusted DCQ is 99.000000. The tolerance itself is not subtracted from the DCQ, and neither is the gas made available.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-daily-balance', 7, 'The buyer nominates 150 against a MaxDCQ of 120 (a DCQ of 100) and the seller makes nothing available. What seller shortfall does the engine return?', '["150.000000, the whole nomination that the seller failed to meet on the day.", "100.000000, since a seller shortfall is measured against the DCQ of the day.", "120.000000.", "30.000000, the part of the nomination that ran above the ceiling of MaxDCQ."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine''s reasons, verbatim: "2027-03-01: nominated 150 is above the MaxDCQ 120; 30 is not properly nominated" and "2027-03-01: the seller made 0 available against a properly nominated 120: seller shortfall 120". The 30 above MaxDCQ carries no obligation, and the yardstick is the properly nominated quantity, which here is above the DCQ.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-daily-balance', 8, 'A golden day has a properly nominated 100, only 40 made available, and force majeure of 30 with maintenance of 10. How much of the 60 not made available is a seller shortfall?', '["60.000000, all of it.", "20.000000, as force majeure and maintenance excuse 40 of the 60.", "0.000000, all excused.", "40.000000, the rest."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine''s reasons, verbatim: "2027-03-01: 40 not made available is excused by the force majeure and maintenance quantities stated for the day" and "2027-03-01: the seller made 40 available against a properly nominated 100: seller shortfall 20". The adjusted DCQ is 100 - 30 - 10 - 20 = 40.000000, and the buyer took all 40, so it has no buyer shortfall.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-daily-balance', 9, 'On 2027-01-12 the power plant states force majeure of 21000, the whole DCQ. What does the engine print for the day?', '["A buyer shortfall of 21000.000000, the same figure the zero nomination of 2027-01-05 returns for its day.", "\"force majeure and maintenance cover the whole DCQ; no quantity is owed either way for the day\"", "A seller shortfall of 21000.000000, paid at the stated damages rate.", "Refused: force majeure may not cover the whole DCQ."]'::jsonb, 1, 'That is the engine''s reason for 2027-01-12, and 2027-01-13 carries the same. Force majeure and maintenance lift the gap off both sides: the adjusted DCQ is 0.000000 and neither shortfall arises. The buyer shortfall of 21000 on 2027-01-05 is a zero NOMINATION on an ordinary day. Force majeure up to the DCQ is accepted; only force majeure and maintenance ABOVE the DCQ are refused.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-daily-balance', 10, 'On a golden day the seller makes 100 available against a properly nominated 100 and the buyer takes 60. What does the engine return, under the reading it states in its basis?', '["A seller shortfall of 0.000000 and a buyer shortfall of 40.000000.", "A seller shortfall of 40.000000 and no buyer shortfall, as the model agreement''s formula subtracts the gas taken.", "A seller shortfall of 40.000000 and a buyer shortfall of 40.000000, the gap counted once on each side.", "No shortfall on either side, settled as over-take."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s basis states the reading: "seller shortfall measured against the quantity the seller made available". The model agreement''s printed formula, SFQ = (PNQ − DTQ ) − DAQ, subtracts the Daily Actual Quantity, which would put the 40 on the seller. The key is what the engine returns under its own stated choice, set beside the text it reads; the reading is the engine''s, and no capstone figure depends on it. Over-take is gas taken above the adjusted DCQ, which this day has none of.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-daily-balance', 11, 'The power plant''s 2027-01-30 is flagged buyerCaused: 21000 properly nominated, 12600 made available and taken. What does the engine return for the day?', '["A seller shortfall of 8400.000000, since the seller made only 12600 available.", "A seller shortfall and a buyer shortfall that split the 8400 between the two sides.", "A buyer shortfall of 8400.000000.", "No shortfall at all, as a buyer-caused gap is excused like force majeure."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine''s reasons, verbatim: "2027-01-30: 8400 of the properly nominated quantity was not made available for a cause on the buyer''s side, so it is not a seller shortfall" and "2027-01-30: taken 12600 is below the adjusted DCQ 21000: buyer shortfall 8400". The flag puts the gap on the buyer; the adjusted DCQ stays 21000.000000, and force majeure is a different excuse that lifts the gap off both sides.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-daily-balance', 12, 'Nothing at all is nominated on 2027-01-05, an ordinary day. How does the daily balance treat it?', '["A refusal, as a nomination of zero is no nomination.", "Nothing owed either way, as for force majeure.", "A seller shortfall of 21000.000000.", "A buyer shortfall of the whole adjusted DCQ, 21000.000000, and no seller shortfall."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine''s reason, verbatim: "2027-01-05: zero nomination; the whole adjusted DCQ 21000 is a buyer shortfall for the day". A zero nomination is lawful and leaves the seller owing nothing, since nothing was properly nominated. The day is ordinary, so the adjusted DCQ is the full DCQ, and the buyer''s gap is carried to the year''s reconciliation.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-daily-balance', 13, 'Force majeure of 80 plus maintenance of 30 are entered against a daily contract quantity of 100. Outcome?', '["A refusal: together they sit above the DCQ.", "A seller shortfall of 0 and an adjusted DCQ clamped at zero, with a reason for the excess.", "A negative adjusted DCQ, which the engine carries into the month''s totals.", "A trimmed force majeure result."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s own words: "days[0].forceMajeure must be a quantity that with maintenance 30 is at or below the DCQ 100; got 80". A quantity that cannot be true is refused before anything is computed. The engine neither clamps, trims nor carries a negative adjusted DCQ for this case.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-daily-balance', 14, 'A learner types "yes" for buyerCaused on one day. What does the engine return?', '["Yes read as true.", "The flag ignored, as usual.", "A refusal naming days[0].buyercaused as an unknown key.", "A refusal: buyerCaused must be true or false."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine''s own words: "days[0].buyerCaused must be true or false when given; got "yes"". The flag takes only true or false, and the engine guesses nothing. The key itself is spelt correctly, so the refusal is about its value and names days[0].buyerCaused.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-daily-balance', 15, 'Scheduled maintenance of 10500 sits on 2027-01-28, and the buyer nominates and takes exactly 10500. How does the engine settle that day?', '["A buyer shortfall of 10500.000000, the part of the DCQ left untaken on the day.", "A seller shortfall of 10500.000000.", "The maintenance leaves 10500.000000 to take, and all of it was taken.", "An adjusted DCQ of 21000.000000 with 10500 excused from the buyer''s side at the year''s end."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The adjusted DCQ is the DCQ less force majeure, maintenance and seller shortfall: 21000 less 10500 is 10500.000000. The buyer took all 10500, so its buyer shortfall is 0.000000. Maintenance excuses the seller, so no seller shortfall arises either; the month''s maintenance total is 10500.000000, this one day.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-take-or-pay-basics', 1, 'Which quantities does the engine take off the ACQ to reach the Adjusted ACQ?', '["The buyer shortfall summed over the year''s days, less the over-take of the same days.", "Force majeure alone.", "Maintenance, force majeure, seller shortfall and any permitted reduction.", "Maintenance and force majeure, with seller shortfall paid as damages and left in."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The rule, in the engine''s basis: Adjusted ACQ = ACQ - maintenance - force majeure - seller shortfall - permitted reduction. The model agreement''s definition subtracts "the Scheduled Maintenance Quantities, Force Majeure Quantities, Shortfall Quantities, and if applicable less the Operational Flexibility Credit". A seller shortfall both reduces the Adjusted ACQ and, where a rate is stated, earns damages. The buyer''s own gaps are what the take-or-pay quantity is measured against, so they never reduce it.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-take-or-pay-basics', 2, 'The power plant''s 2027, run alone, carries force majeure of 42000 and a seller shortfall of 6300 against an ACQ of 7665000. What Adjusted ACQ does the engine return?', '["7616700.000000, the ACQ less reductions of 48300.000000.", "7665000.000000, since reductions only lower the take-or-pay quantity.", "7245000.000000, the gas the buyer actually took that year.", "6093360.000000."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Force majeure of 42000 and a seller shortfall of 6300 make reductions of 48300.000000, and 7665000 less that is 7616700.000000. These are the planted 2027 situation the take-or-pay year finds. 7245000.000000 is the year''s take, and 6093360.000000 is the take-or-pay quantity, 80 percent of the Adjusted ACQ.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-take-or-pay-basics', 3, 'What take-or-pay quantity does the engine return for the power plant''s 2027 run alone, at 80 percent?', '["6132000.000000, on the full ACQ.", "6093360.000000, 80 percent of the Adjusted ACQ.", "6148800.000000, the leap-year figure.", "7616700.000000, the Adjusted ACQ itself, which the buyer must take in full to avoid a deficiency."]'::jsonb, 1, 'TOPQ = topPct % of Adjusted ACQ, and 80 percent of 7616700.000000 is 6093360.000000. The model agreement defines the Take or Pay Quantity as a percentage "of the Adjusted Annual Contract Quantity for that Contract Year". 6132000.000000 applies the percentage before the year''s reductions, a planning figure; 6148800.000000 belongs to the leap years 2028 and 2032.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-take-or-pay-basics', 4, 'Run alone, 2027 saw the power plant''s buyer take 7245000, comfortably past the 6093360.000000 it owed. Which deficiency results?', '["The gas taken above the take-or-pay quantity, carried to the buyer''s credit for later years.", "The ACQ less the gas taken, which the buyer pays for at the take-or-pay price.", "48300.000000, the reductions, which the buyer owes as the year''s deficiency in their place.", "0.000000."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine''s rule: deficiency = TOPQ - (taken - make-up taken) when positive. The take is above the take-or-pay quantity, so the deficiency is 0.000000 and the deficiency payment 0.000000. Nothing in a single year credits the excess to the buyer, the yardstick is the take-or-pay quantity, and reductions protect the buyer.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-take-or-pay-basics', 5, 'A golden year has an ACQ of 1000 with reductions of 200, take-or-pay at 80 percent, 600 taken and a take-or-pay price of 3. What deficiency payment does the engine return?', '["120.000000, for a deficiency of 40 at 3.", "600.000000, for a deficiency of 200 against 80 percent of the full ACQ.", "75.000000, the seller shortfall damages of that same year.", "1845.000000."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Adjusted ACQ is 800.000000 and the take-or-pay quantity 640.000000, so 600 taken leaves a deficiency of 40.000000; the engine''s reason opens: "2027: 600 counted against the take-or-pay quantity 640 leaves a deficiency of 40; the deficiency payment is 40 x 3 = 120". Skipping the reductions gives the wrong 600. 75.000000 is that year''s seller shortfall damages and 1845.000000 its net to the seller.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-take-or-pay-basics', 6, 'Ninety percent take-or-pay on an ACQ of 1000, and only 500 taken in the case''s one year: find the deficiency.', '["500.000000, the ACQ less the gas the buyer took.", "1200.000000, the year''s full deficiency.", "400.000000.", "800.000000, taking 80 percent as a default."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The take-or-pay quantity is 90 percent of the Adjusted ACQ of 1000, 900.000000, and 900 less 500 counted is 400.000000. The yardstick is the take-or-pay quantity, which leaves out the ACQ above it. 1200.000000 is the deficiency PAYMENT at the take-or-pay price of 3, a sum of money. The engine holds no default percentage; topPct is stated.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-take-or-pay-basics', 7, 'A golden year has a take-or-pay quantity of 800 and the buyer takes exactly 800. What does the engine return?', '["A deficiency of 1.000000, because the take must exceed the quantity.", "A deficiency of 0.000000 and no reason, as there is nothing to reconcile.", "A deficiency of 0.000000 with a reason naming a make-up right of 0 opened for the year that follows it.", "A refusal, since a take equal to the take-or-pay quantity needs a tie-break rule stated in the contract."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The deficiency is the take-or-pay quantity less the quantity counted when that is above 0, so taking exactly 800 leaves 0.000000, and the engine returns an empty list of reasons, since nothing is left to reconcile. The model agreement asks the buyer to take "at least" the take-or-pay quantity, and at least includes equal. A deficiency of 1.000000 belongs to the case one unit short.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-take-or-pay-basics', 8, 'One unit short: a take-or-pay quantity of 800, 799 taken, a take-or-pay price of 3. What deficiency payment does the engine return?', '["0.000000, as one unit is within any rounding of the take-or-pay quantity.", "1.000000, the deficiency quantity, read as the payment.", "2400.000000, the whole take-or-pay quantity at 3, since the quantity was missed.", "3.000000."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine''s reason opens, verbatim: "2027: 799 counted against the take-or-pay quantity 800 leaves a deficiency of 1; the deficiency payment is 1 x 3 = 3". The engine compares exact numbers, with no rounding band. 2400.000000 is the payment of a year in which nothing was taken.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-take-or-pay-basics', 9, 'A golden year has an ACQ of 1000, a take-or-pay quantity of 800 and a take-or-pay price of 3, and the buyer''s plant stands idle all year, taking no gas. What comes back?', '["A deficiency of 1000.000000, paid at 3000.000000.", "No deficiency, as for force majeure.", "A refusal: nothing taken, nothing to reconcile.", "A deficiency of 800.000000, paid at 2400.000000."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine''s reason opens: "2027: 0 counted against the take-or-pay quantity 800 leaves a deficiency of 800; the deficiency payment is 800 x 3 = 2400". The whole take-or-pay quantity is the deficiency; the ACQ above it is never owed. A zero take is a result, and force majeure is only ever a stated input.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-take-or-pay-basics', 10, 'A golden year states force majeure covering its whole ACQ of 1000. What does the engine return?', '["An Adjusted ACQ of 0.000000, a take-or-pay quantity of 0.000000 and no deficiency.", "A deficiency of 800.000000, since force majeure excuses the seller but leaves the buyer''s obligation.", "A refusal, as reductions may not equal the ACQ.", "An Adjusted ACQ of 1000.000000, force majeure being counted only day by day."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The golden case top-fm-whole-year returns an Adjusted ACQ of 0.000000, a take-or-pay quantity of 0.000000 and no deficiency, with no reason, since nothing is left to reconcile. Reductions may equal the ACQ; only reductions ABOVE it are refused. Force majeure stated for the year comes off the ACQ like any other reduction.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-take-or-pay-basics', 11, 'The power plant''s leap year 2032 is run alone: ACQ 7686000, no reductions, 80 percent, and 6148800 taken. What does the engine find?', '["A deficiency measured from the ACQ of 7686000.000000.", "A deficiency of 0.000000, the take-or-pay quantity exactly met.", "A deficiency, since 2032 is scored on 365 days.", "A deficiency of 0.000000 and an over-take credited forward to the next year of the term."]'::jsonb, 1, 'In 2032 the take-or-pay quantity is 80 percent of 7686000.000000, which is 6148800.000000, and the buyer took exactly that. It is the fixture''s planted boundary: deficiency 0 at the boundary, and the one-year run returns no reason, since nothing is left to reconcile. The ACQ is not the yardstick, 2032 counts 366 days, and a single year carries nothing forward.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-take-or-pay-basics', 12, 'A learner sets topPct to 101 in the one take-or-pay year view. What does the engine return?', '["A take-or-pay quantity above the Adjusted ACQ, with a reason that flags the unusual percentage stated.", "A result clamped at 100 percent.", "A refusal: topPct must be a number from 0 to 100.", "A result at a default 90 percent."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine''s own words: "topPct must be a number from 0 to 100; got 101". The percentage has no default at all, so the engine neither clamps it nor substitutes one. A percentage of 0 is accepted and gives a take-or-pay quantity of zero.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-take-or-pay-basics', 13, 'A take-or-pay year is entered with no topPrice. What does the engine return?', '["A refusal: the take-or-pay price has no default.", "A result at the contract price, copied across.", "A deficiency payment of 0.000000.", "A result at the reported domestic base price."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s own words: "years[0].topPrice must be a finite number at or above 0; got nothing". The deficiency payment is (deficiency - carry-forward credit) x topPrice, and a contract may state the take-or-pay price apart from the contract price, so the engine copies nothing. The domestic base price is a required input of the domestic price function only, quoted only as reported.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-take-or-pay-basics', 14, 'A year states an ACQ of 100 with maintenance, force majeure, seller shortfall and a permitted reduction adding to 110. What does the engine return?', '["An Adjusted ACQ of 0.000000, the reductions capped at the ACQ.", "An Adjusted ACQ below zero and a negative take-or-pay quantity, with a reason naming the excess.", "A result that ignores the permitted reduction, the last term in the sum, until the total fits.", "A refusal naming years[0].acq."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine''s own words: "years[0].acq must be at or above the reductions it carries (maintenance + force majeure + seller shortfall + permitted reduction = 110); got 100". Reductions larger than the year''s ACQ describe an impossible year, so the call stops before any figure is produced; nothing is capped and no term is dropped.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-take-or-pay-basics', 15, 'How does this course describe the money a buyer owes on a deficiency?', '["A fine the Commission levies per MMBtu under the Act.", "A deficiency payment: payment for gas committed to and not taken.", "Damages the buyer pays for a seller shortfall.", "An over-take charge on days above the adjusted DCQ."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The vocabulary rule: a deficiency is the take-or-pay quantity less the quantity counted, when above zero, and the money is the deficiency payment. Article 12.6 obliges the buyer "to take and pay for, or to pay for if not taken" the take-or-pay quantity, so it pays for gas it committed to. The course keeps the word penalty for the Act''s Domestic Gas Delivery Obligation, and damages for the seller''s side.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-reading-one-contract-year', 1, 'As the engine builds it, which sum gives the net to the seller for a contract year?', '["Taken x contract price + deficiency payment + seller shortfall x shortfall price, the damages being added to the seller''s side.", "Counted x contract price + make-up taken x make-up price + deficiency payment - seller shortfall x shortfall price - refund.", "ACQ x contract price - deficiency payment - seller shortfall x shortfall price, as the buyer pays for the ACQ.", "Take-or-pay quantity x take-or-pay price, whatever the buyer takes, less any refund at the end of the term."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine''s money line: counted x contract price + make-up taken x make-up price + deficiency payment - seller shortfall x shortfall price - refund = net to the seller, each term a return value. Seller shortfall damages are paid BY the seller, so they come off. The buyer never pays for the ACQ as such, and gas taken above the take-or-pay quantity is paid for at the contract price, so the seller''s year is no flat take-or-pay sum.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-reading-one-contract-year', 2, 'The single-year golden case counts 500 at a contract price of 3 and returns a deficiency payment of 1200. What net to the seller does the engine return?', '["1200.000000, the deficiency payment alone, as the gas counted is paid for separately.", "2400.000000, the take-or-pay quantity of 800 at the price of 3 for the year.", "3000.000000, the whole ACQ of 1000 at the price of 3, as the buyer''s full year.", "2700.000000."]'::jsonb, 3, 'In one year with no make-up, damages or refund, the net is counted x contract price + deficiency payment: the counted gas at 3 plus the 1200.000000 paid for the rest of the take-or-pay quantity of 900, which the engine returns as 2700.000000. That case''s take-or-pay quantity is 900 at 90 percent, and the ACQ is paid for whole only in a year the buyer takes all of it.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-reading-one-contract-year', 3, 'The exactly-met golden year and the zero-take golden year both return a net to the seller of 2400.000000. Why?', '["Up to the take-or-pay quantity the seller is paid whether the gas flows or not.", "The engine fills a zero-take year with the take-or-pay quantity, as if taken.", "Both years hold the same deficiency payment of 2400.000000.", "Both are last years, and the net is fixed at the end of term."]'::jsonb, 0, 'In the exactly-met year 800 is counted at 3 with no deficiency payment; in the zero-take year nothing is counted and the deficiency payment is 2400.000000. Each nets 2400.000000. That is take-or-pay working as designed; above the take-or-pay quantity the seller is paid only for what the buyer takes. The engine records the zero take as 0.000000 counted.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-reading-one-contract-year', 4, 'Force majeure and a seller shortfall hit one golden year: 600 counted at 3, a deficiency payment of 120, damages of 75. How much nets to the seller?', '["120.000000, the deficiency payment, since the counted gas and the damages are settled apart.", "2400.000000, as reductions leave the seller''s year at the take-or-pay quantity of 800 at 3.", "1845.000000.", "1845.000000 plus 75.000000, as the damages are added back on the seller''s side of the sum."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Counted x contract price + deficiency payment - seller shortfall x shortfall price: the 600 counted at 3, plus 120, less 75, is 1845.000000 as the engine returns it. The damages are paid by the seller, so they reduce its net; the reductions lower the Adjusted ACQ to 800.000000 and the take-or-pay quantity to 640.000000.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-reading-one-contract-year', 5, 'The same golden year states a seller shortfall of 50 at a shortfall price of 1.5. What does the engine''s reason say?', '["The 50 is added to the buyer''s deficiency and paid to the seller at 1.5, a sum of 75.", "A line saying the 50 reduces the ACQ alone, leaving the take-or-pay quantity unchanged, with no payment.", "That the 50 is paid to the buyer at 1.5, and the Adjusted ACQ stays whole.", "\"2027: seller shortfall 50 reduces the Adjusted ACQ and is paid to the buyer at 1.5: 75\""]'::jsonb, 3, 'The quoted line is the engine''s own words, and it names both consequences of one seller shortfall: it reduces the Adjusted ACQ, so the take-or-pay quantity falls with it, and it is paid to the buyer at the stated rate. The damages run from the seller to the buyer, and the ACQ itself never changes.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-reading-one-contract-year', 6, 'A year states a seller shortfall of 5 and no shortfallPrice. What does the engine return?', '["Damages of 0.000000, as no rate was stated.", "A refusal, because the engine holds no default rate.", "A result at the power plant''s 1.25 rate.", "A result at the take-or-pay price, the nearest stated price."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine''s own words: "years[0].shortfallPrice must be stated when sellerShortfall is above 0 (5); the engine holds no default rate; got nothing". The rate is a stated contract term. The power plant''s 1.25 US$ per MMBtu is that synthetic agreement''s own term, and the engine borrows no other price.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-reading-one-contract-year', 7, 'When the power plant''s 2027 is reconciled on its own, how much does the seller owe in damages, and at which rate?', '["7875.000000: the 6300 seller shortfall at the fixture''s stated 1.25 US$ per MMBtu, a synthetic contract term.", "7875.000000, at a rate the PIA sets for every domestic power sector agreement in Nigeria.", "6300.000000, one US$ per MMBtu of seller shortfall under the model agreement.", "48300.000000, the year''s reductions at the stated rate."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s reason, verbatim: "2027: seller shortfall 6300 reduces the Adjusted ACQ and is paid to the buyer at 1.25: 7875". The fixture''s note reads "seller shortfall liquidated damages at a stated 1.25 US$ per MMBtu not made available (synthetic contract term)". The rate is a stated contract term with no default, and force majeure earns no damages.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-reading-one-contract-year', 8, 'The power plant''s 2027 run alone returns a net to the seller of 15786225.000000. Which statement about that figure can the course defend?', '["It is the seller''s revenue for 2027 at the price the Authority determined for that year.", "It forecasts what the buyer will pay in 2027, from the plant demand the fixture states.", "It is what the stated clauses give on the fixture''s planning-assumption price, and no forecast of payment.", "It rests on the commercial sector price of 2.68, as reported, which a power plant pays."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The fixture holds 2.18 US$ per MMBtu, the domestic base price reported for 2026, flat in every year as a stated planning assumption; any money figure on the power plant says so. The regulator''s circular was not read, and the Authority re-determines the price each year, so no 2027 figure exists in this course. A computed figure is what the stated clauses produce on stated takes, and no forecast. PIA s.167(5) prices the power sector at the domestic base price.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-reading-one-contract-year', 9, 'Leap year 2032, run alone, has its take-or-pay quantity exactly met. Which net to the seller comes back, quoted as the course requires?', '["13404384.000000, stated with the price it rests on: 2.18, a planning assumption the fixture holds.", "13404384.000000, with nothing more to say, since the take-or-pay quantity was exactly met by the buyer in 2032.", "13404384.000000 as the seller''s forecast revenue for 2032 at the gazetted domestic base price.", "0.000000, since the deficiency is zero at the boundary."]'::jsonb, 0, 'In 2032 the buyer took exactly the take-or-pay quantity, 6148800.000000, so the deficiency payment is 0.000000 and the net is the counted gas at the contract price, 13404384.000000. The contract price is the fixture''s 2.18, the reported 2026 figure held flat as a stated planning assumption, so the figure is quoted with it; no domestic base price is gazetted in this course.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-reading-one-contract-year', 10, 'A reason prints a figure as the shortest decimal that reads back to the value the engine holds. Which figure does this course quote when it states a result?', '["The reason''s figure, since it is the engine''s own words and carries every digit it holds.", "Whichever of the two is shorter, as long as both agree at the printed precision of the tile.", "The reason''s figure rounded by hand to four decimals, as the model agreement requires.", "The field."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The fields, the numbers in the table and the tiles, are what the course quotes, always at six decimals. A reason may appear only verbatim, as the engine''s own words, and serves to explain a result. The four-decimal rule of the model agreement belongs to contract prices, and printed agreement between two figures proves nothing.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-reading-one-contract-year', 11, 'Nothing unusual happened on 2027-01-11: the buyer asked for, was offered and took the DCQ of 21000. What line appears for that day?', '["A buyer shortfall of 0, as its reason line.", "Its over-take, recorded as zero.", "Nothing: a day with nothing owed, excused or trimmed gets no line.", "A line confirming the DCQ was met exactly and that both sides of the delivery point are square for the day."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The daily balance prints a reason only on a day on which something is owed, excused or trimmed. On 2027-01-11 the buyer shortfall and over-take are both 0.000000, and the engine prints nothing. A day with nothing to say gets no reason, just as a year with nothing to reconcile returns an empty list of reasons.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-reading-one-contract-year', 12, 'What is a liquidated damages rate for a seller shortfall?', '["A rate a court fixes after the event, from the loss the buyer proves.", "A rate the parties agree at signing for each unit not made available.", "The Act''s US$3.50 per MMBtu, applied to every seller shortfall.", "A price the engine derives from the contract price of the day, at a stated share."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Liquidated damages are fixed in advance, so neither side has to prove its actual loss after the event; the rate is the parties'' agreed estimate of what a missing unit costs the buyer, and the engine holds no default. US$3.50 per MMBtu is the Act''s Domestic Gas Delivery Obligation penalty, a different provision taught at the next tier.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-reading-one-contract-year', 13, 'A seller shortfall reaches the year''s reconciliation with a stated rate. What two things does it do?', '["It adds to the buyer''s deficiency.", "It reduces the ACQ itself and cancels the buyer''s deficiency payment for the whole of the year.", "It opens a make-up right for the seller and reduces the buyer''s take-or-pay price for the year.", "It reduces the Adjusted ACQ and earns the buyer damages."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A seller shortfall reduces the Adjusted ACQ, so the take-or-pay quantity falls with it and the buyer is never made to pay for gas that was not offered; and where the contract states a rate, the seller pays the buyer damages, which the net to the seller subtracts. The ACQ, the deficiency rule and the prices are untouched, and make-up is gas the BUYER paid for.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-reading-one-contract-year', 14, 'What separates a reason from a refusal in what the engine returns?', '["A reason explains a figure; a refusal returns no figures at all.", "A reason is printed for an error the engine repaired; a refusal is printed for one it could not repair.", "A reason is a warning, and figures beside it may not be quoted.", "A refusal lists the figures the engine could compute before it stopped."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A reason sits beside a result: a deficiency with no make-up right, a day trimmed at MaxDCQ, a day excused by force majeure. A refusal names the field it refused and the exact condition that failed, and nothing is computed. The engine repairs nothing: an input it cannot read is refused by name.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-reading-one-contract-year', 15, 'A year states a contract price of 3 and a different take-or-pay price. Which price sets the deficiency payment?', '["The contract price, which prices every MMBtu of the year, taken or paid for.", "The higher of the two prices, which protects the seller against a buyer that takes less.", "The take-or-pay price.", "An average of the two prices, weighted by the gas counted and the deficiency."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The rule, in the engine''s basis: deficiency payment = (deficiency - carry-forward credit) x topPrice. The contract price prices the counted gas; the take-or-pay price, a stated input with no default, prices the deficiency. A contract may state the two apart, and the engine uses each for its own term with no maximum or average.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 1, 'Which gazette reference belongs to the Petroleum Industry Act 2021 itself?', '["Official Gazette No. 206, Vol. 109, 23 November 2022, which amended the Act.", "Act No. 6, Official Gazette No. 142, Vol. 108, 27 August 2021.", "The statutory instrument the Act was made under, S.I. No. 74.", "An unnumbered, undated copy on a regulator''s page."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Act is Act No. 6, printed in Official Gazette No. 142, Vol. 108, dated 27 August 2021, and read for this course on 2026-09-26. Gazette No. 206 and S.I. No. 74 are the references of the 2022 Regulations made under the Act; an Act is not made under a statutory instrument. The unnumbered, undated copy describes the flaring Regulations of 2023.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 2, 'Which edition of the ESMAP report on long-term gas contracts does the course read?', '["ESMAP Report 152/93 in its 2025 revision, published under Creative Commons Attribution 4.0.", "The edition current as of October 2017, published jointly with the US Department of Energy.", "The 2007 edition, whose pricing chapter prints Figure 51 and the heat-equivalence slope.", "ESMAP Report 152/93, January 1993, World Bank and UNDP."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The course names ESMAP Report 152/93, Long-term Gas Contracts: Principles and Applications, January 1993, World Bank and UNDP, read on 2026-09-26. The 2025 Creative Commons text is the Commonwealth model gas sales agreement; October 2017 is the CLDP and US DOE guide; 2007 is the Energy Charter Secretariat''s pricing study.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 3, 'What licence and date does the course record for the two HMRC Oil Taxation Manual pages it reads?', '["Both updated 19 December 2019, under the Open Government Licence.", "Licensed text, taught by concept only.", "Creative Commons Attribution 4.0, in the 2025 edition of the model contract series.", "Gazetted on 27 August 2021 beside the Act."]'::jsonb, 0, 'OT05435 and OT05402 are both updated 19 December 2019, Open Government Licence, and read on 2026-09-26, so the course quotes them with citation. The licensed text taught by concept only is the AIPN model agreement. Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 is the Commonwealth model agreement''s licence, and a UK tax manual is no part of the Nigerian gazette.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 4, 'The oil-indexed Ekene contract runs from 2027 to 2036. What are its daily and take-or-pay terms?', '["A DCQ of 21000 MMBtu per day from 20 MMscf at 1050 Btu/scf, MaxDCQ 110 percent, take-or-pay 80 percent.", "A DCQ of 63000 MMBtu per day from 60 MMscf at 1050 Btu/scf, MaxDCQ 110 percent, take-or-pay 80 percent.", "A DCQ of 63000 MMBtu per day from 60 MMscf at 1050 Btu/scf, MaxDCQ 105 percent, take-or-pay 90 percent.", "A DCQ of 60 MMscf per day with no heating value stated, since the buyer takes feed gas by volume."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The export feed fixture states a DCQ of 63000 MMBtu per day (60 MMscf per day at 1050 Btu/scf), MaxDCQ 105 percent and take-or-pay 90 percent, running 2027 to 2036. The 21000 MMBtu, 110 percent and 80 percent terms are the power plant''s. Both fixtures state their DCQ in energy with the heating value it came from.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 5, 'The power plant fixture plants a situation in 2032 for the take-or-pay year to find. Which one?', '["A plant outage leaving a paid deficiency.", "Force majeure of 42,000 and a seller shortfall of 6,300 reducing the Adjusted ACQ.", "A take that lands precisely on the year''s obligation, found at the boundary.", "A zero nomination and a buyer-caused gap."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The fixture README''s table reads "2032: the take-or-pay quantity exactly met", found by "takeOrPay deficiency 0 at the boundary". The plant outage with a deficiency is 2028, force majeure of 42,000 with a seller shortfall of 6,300 is 2027, and the zero nomination and buyer-caused gap are days of January 2027.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 6, 'What does the gas contract engine import, and from where?', '["npv, deriveGasRoyaltyRate and calendarDays, from engines/economics/cashflow.ts.", "A Monte Carlo sampler from the statistics library.", "The domestic base price, from the regulator''s circular.", "Nothing at all: it carries its own NPV and royalty code alongside its contract rules."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s one import is engines/economics/cashflow.ts, for npv, deriveGasRoyaltyRate and calendarDays. It carries no NPV, Monte Carlo or royalty code of its own, nothing in it samples, it makes no network call, and the domestic base price is a required input with no default.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 7, 'A day entry in a dailyBalance call spells forcemajeure in lower case. What does the engine return?', '["A result reading it as forceMajeure.", "Force majeure taken as 0, with a reason.", "A result in which the whole day counts as force majeure until the key is fixed.", "A refusal naming days[0].forcemajeure and listing the keys a day accepts."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine''s own words: "days[0].forcemajeure is not an accepted key; the accepted keys of days[0] are date, nominated, available, taken, forceMajeure, maintenance, buyerCaused". An input key a function does not read is refused at whatever level it sits, with its path and the accepted keys, so no term is ever silently dropped or guessed.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 8, 'Sixty million standard cubic feet a day of the export feed''s gas, at its stated 1050 Btu/scf gross: which two energy figures does the view print?', '["66468.518715 MMBtu and 63000.000000 GJ, the two tiles reading the other way.", "63000.000000 MMBtu and 66468.518715 GJ.", "63000.000000 MMBtu and 63000.000000 GJ, a gross figure needing no constant.", "21000.000000 MMBtu and 22156.172905 GJ, the per-train figure of the export DCQ."]'::jsonb, 1, 'On the imperial route the MMBtu tile is the plain product divided by a million, so the export DCQ is 63000.000000 MMBtu; the gigajoule tile applies 1055.05585262 MJ per MMBtu and prints 66468.518715. The pair 21000.000000 and 22156.172905 belongs to the power plant''s 20 MMscf, and swapping or dropping the constant gives the other two options.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 9, 'A small meter reading of 1000 scf carries a heating value quoted as 39 MJ/Sm3 gross. What energy results?', '["39.000000 MMBtu, as the scf volume is read as Sm3 by the engine without any conversion.", "1.104357 MMBtu, the product of the volume and the heating value with no Btu constant.", "A refusal, since the engine takes a metric heating value only with a metric volume.", "1.046728 MMBtu."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine first turns standard cubic feet into standard cubic metres by the exact cubic-foot factor, applies the megajoule heating value, then divides by the megajoules in one MMBtu: 1.046728 MMBtu, or 1.104357 GJ. That GJ figure is what skipping the last division gives. Unlike units are accepted together, with one set of reference conditions assumed.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 10, 'What separates the gross heating value of a gas from its net heating value?', '["Gross counts the heat of condensing the water that burning makes; net leaves it out.", "Gross is measured at 60 F and net at 15 C.", "Gross includes the heat of any liquids removed at the plant; net counts the dry gas only.", "Gross comes before the Btu constant, net after it."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Burning natural gas makes water. The gross, or higher, heating value counts the heat recovered when that water condenses; the net, or lower, value leaves it out. The difference is chemistry: reference conditions are a separate statement, and the Btu constant converts units without changing either basis.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 11, 'The cubic-foot-to-cubic-metre factor, 0.028316846592, rests on what?', '["A measured volume at 15 C and 101.325 kPa, updated with each edition of NIST SP 811.", "The ratio of the power plant''s 60 F conditions to the golden cases'' 15 C conditions.", "(0.3048 m) cubed, exact.", "A rounding the EIA energy calculator prints, read for this course on 2026-09-26."]'::jsonb, 2, 'UNITS.M3_PER_FT3 is 0.028316846592, (0.3048 m) cubed, exact. It is pure geometry, so a mixed pair applies it and makes no correction for temperature or pressure; it has nothing to do with either set of reference conditions or with any published calculator.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 12, 'Both Ekene fixtures state their heating value on one basis. Which, and why does it matter?', '["Net, as no heat is recovered from the water.", "Gross; every figure built on the DCQ inherits that basis.", "Gross for the power plant and net for the export feed, each buyer choosing its own basis.", "Neither; the engine ignores the basis."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Both fixtures state 1050 Btu/scf gross. Every quantity downstream of the DCQ carries the basis, so a gross DCQ cannot be compared with a take converted on a net basis until one is restated. The engine does the same arithmetic on either value, which is exactly why the stated basis must be the right one: the label travels with the number.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 13, 'How should a learner quote the power plant''s daily contract quantity in energy?', '["21000 MMBtu, with no basis needed for a daily figure.", "22156.172905 MMBtu, net.", "20 MMscf, the volume, since energy is only an invoice unit and has no place in a contract.", "21000.000000 MMBtu, on a gross basis."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The course quotes every energy figure with its basis, at six decimals: the power plant''s DCQ is 21000.000000 MMBtu on a gross basis. 22156.172905 is that energy in GJ on the same gross basis. The contract counts quantity in energy; the volume is where the energy came from.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 14, 'Zero cubic metres go into the volume-to-energy view. Refused, or returned?', '["Returned: 0.000000 MMBtu and 0.000000 GJ, with the rule printed as usual.", "Refused: quantity must be a finite number above 0.", "Refused, naming referenceConditions, since no gas was measured.", "Returned blank, with a reason saying no gas was delivered."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The golden case energy-zero-quantity returns 0.000000 MMBtu and 0.000000 GJ with the metric rule line. The refusal is for a negative quantity: the engine''s words are "quantity must be a finite number at or above 0; got -1". A zero quantity is a result.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 15, 'Stated as running from 1 January 2027 up to 1 January 2028, how long is the export feed''s contract year, and what does it hold?', '["23058000.000000 MMBtu over 366 days, both dates counted.", "A refusal, as a period stated this way must stop on 31 December of its year.", "22995000.000000 MMBtu over 365 days, the closing date left out.", "7665000.000000 MMBtu over 365 days, at the power plant''s DCQ."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine counts a period from its first date up to its closing date and leaves the closing date out, which is how a contract year that finishes on the following 1 January is written. That gives 365 days, and at 63000 a day 22995000.000000. The leap-year export figure is 23058000.000000, and 7665000.000000 belongs to the power plant.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 16, 'At a DCQ of 1000, what does calendar year 2000 give?', '["366 days and an ACQ of 366000.000000: a century year divisible by four hundred.", "365 days and 365000.000000, because century years are never leap years.", "A refusal, since 2000 lies before the Act.", "366 days, but an ACQ of 365000.000000."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s day count reads "calendar year 2000: 366 days (a leap year)", and 1000 x 366 is 366000.000000. A century year is a leap year only when it divides by four hundred, which 2000 does and 2100 does not. A calendar year carries no legal date limit, and the ACQ is always the DCQ times the days counted.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 17, 'HMRC''s own example states a DCQ of 100, 365 days, MaxDCQ 150 percent and take-or-pay 90 percent. What MaxDCQ and swing factor does the engine return?', '["1.666667 and 1.500000, since MaxDCQ is quoted as a ratio.", "36500.000000 and 1.666667, the ACQ and the effective swing.", "150.000000 and 1.666667, the swing factor divided by the take-or-pay fraction.", "150.000000 and 1.500000."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The golden case returns MaxDCQ 150.000000 (150 percent of 100), swing factor 1.500000, ACQ 36500.000000, take-or-pay quantity 32850.000000 and effective swing 1.666667. The swing factor is MaxDCQ / DCQ; dividing it by the take-or-pay fraction gives the EFFECTIVE swing, a different figure.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 18, 'A learner types "2027-02-30" as the first day of a contract year. What does the engine return?', '["A period starting on 2027-03-02, the date rolled forward by the calendar.", "A refusal: period.start must be a real date.", "A period starting on 2027-02-28, the last real day of that February in 2027.", "A result for 365 days, the period read as calendar year 2027."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine''s own words: "period.start must be a real date ''YYYY-MM-DD''; got "2027-02-30"". A date that does not exist on the calendar is refused before anything is counted. The engine neither rolls a date forward nor back, and a period never becomes a calendar year.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 19, 'A contract''s effective swing rises. Which side does that favour, according to the course?', '["The seller, whose facilities then run at a steadier daily load through the contract year.", "The buyer, who may take much on busy days and little on quiet ones and still meet its obligation.", "Neither side: effective swing enters every deficiency payment the engine computes.", "The regulator, since effective swing fixes the domestic gas delivery obligation."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A higher effective swing gives the buyer more room: it can nominate up to MaxDCQ on peak days and take little on others while still reaching its take-or-pay level. A lower one lets the seller run closer to a steady load. Nothing in the engine turns effective swing into a quantity or a payment; it is a measure for comparing contracts and sizing facilities.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 20, 'Fifty thousand a day for 360 stated days, take-or-pay set to zero, no MaxDCQ at all: what comes back?', '["A refusal for a take-or-pay percentage of 0.", "18000000.000000 for the ACQ and an effective swing of 0.000000 beside it.", "An ACQ of 17500000.000000, read as 350 days.", "18000000.000000 for the ACQ, with nothing owed under take-or-pay."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The golden case cq-top-zero-without-swing returns an ACQ of 18000000.000000, a take-or-pay quantity of 0.000000 and no effective swing. A zero percentage is refused only when a MaxDCQ is stated, because the effective swing would divide by it. 17500000.000000 is the 350-day case.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 21, 'In how many ways, and which, may a contractQuantities call state its day count?', '["Two: a calendar year or a stated number of days, a period being refused as too loose.", "One: a calendar year, since every contract year runs from 1 January to 31 December.", "Three: a stated number of days, a calendar year, or a period whose end date is excluded.", "Any mix of the three, the engine summing them into one count for the ACQ."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine takes days, year or period, exactly one of them. A period''s end date is excluded, as a contract year that finishes on the following 1 January. Two counts at once are refused, and so is none, so the ACQ never rests on a count the contract did not state.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 22, 'A golden day is flagged buyerCaused: 100 properly nominated, 20 made available. In whose column does the missing 80 land, and why?', '["The buyer''s, because the flag places the cause on its side.", "The seller''s, as it made only 20 of the 100 available.", "Neither column; the engine treats the flag like force majeure.", "Both columns, split between the buyer and the seller."]'::jsonb, 0, 'With buyerCaused set true the engine counts no seller shortfall, keeps the adjusted DCQ at 100 and books the 80 as buyer shortfall. Without the flag the same day would be the seller''s failure, and force majeure, a different input, would lift the gap off both sides. The flag takes true or false only.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 23, 'The January 2027 daily balance prints an ACQ for the days of 651000.000000. Where does that figure come from?', '["The Adjusted ACQ plus buyer shortfall.", "The sum of the month''s nominations, capped each day at MaxDCQ, less the over-take.", "The 31 days of January at the DCQ of 21000.", "One twelfth of the year''s ACQ, 7665000.000000, rounded to the whole day."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The ACQ for the days is 31 days at the DCQ, 651000.000000. Take off the maintenance (10500.000000), the force majeure (42000.000000) and the seller shortfall (6300.000000) and you have the Adjusted ACQ for the days, 592200.000000. Nominations and takes do not enter the ACQ, and the engine never divides the year.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 24, 'On 2027-01-25 the power plant''s buyer took 23100 against a DCQ of 21000. What over-take does the engine return for the day?', '["1050.000000, the part of the nomination above MaxDCQ that was never properly nominated.", "2100.000000.", "23100.000000, the whole of the day''s take, all of it counted as over-take.", "0.000000, since gas taken within MaxDCQ is never counted as over-take by the engine."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Over-take is the gas taken above the adjusted DCQ: 23100 less 21000 is 2100.000000. The 1050 above MaxDCQ was never properly nominated or made available, so it was not taken either. Over-take is carried to the month''s totals, where it offsets buyer shortfall in the reconciliation identity.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 25, 'A dailyBalance call lists 2027-03-02 and then 2027-03-02 again. What does the engine return?', '["Both entries merged into one day''s figures.", "A result that keeps the later entry and drops the first.", "A result for both entries, with a reason naming the repeat.", "A refusal: each day''s date must come after the one before."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine''s own words: "days[1].date must be after the previous day 2027-03-02; got "2027-03-02"". Days must be real dates in ascending order, and a quantity or a sequence that cannot be true is refused before anything is computed. The engine never merges, drops or keeps a duplicate day.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 26, 'In which order does the engine''s rule build a day''s seller shortfall?', '["The properly nominated quantity, less the tolerance, less the gas made available, less the force majeure and maintenance of the day.", "The DCQ less the gas taken, less the tolerance, with force majeure then added back.", "The nomination less the gas taken, before any cap at MaxDCQ is applied to it.", "The adjusted DCQ less the gas made available, with the tolerance ignored."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The rule, in the engine''s basis: seller shortfall = (PNQ - tolerance) - available, less the force majeure and maintenance stated for the day, none on a buyer-caused day. The yardstick is the properly nominated quantity, which is the nomination capped at MaxDCQ, and the engine measures against the gas made available as its stated reading.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 27, 'Force majeure of 100 blankets a day on which the buyer still nominated 100 and nothing flowed. What comes back?', '["Seller liable for all 100, the nomination unmet.", "A buyer shortfall of 100.000000, the nomination left untaken.", "Nothing owed by either side; the adjusted DCQ falls to 0.000000.", "A refusal, as a nomination on a force majeure day is contradictory and the engine cannot reconcile it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine''s reasons, verbatim: "2027-03-01: 100 not made available is excused by the force majeure and maintenance quantities stated for the day" and "2027-03-01: force majeure and maintenance cover the whole DCQ; no quantity is owed either way for the day". A nomination may stand on such a day; force majeure lifts the gap off both sides.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 28, 'The power plant''s January 2027 totals print force majeure of 42000.000000. Which days carry it?', '["2027-01-20, the day the seller made only 15750 available.", "2027-01-28, the maintenance day.", "Every day of the month, spread evenly across the thirty-one of them.", "2027-01-12 and 2027-01-13, each with 21000."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The day table shows force majeure of 21000.000000 on 2027-01-12 and on 2027-01-13, two whole days, 42000.000000 in all. 2027-01-20 is the seller shortfall of 6300.000000, and 2027-01-28 carries maintenance of 10500.000000, a separate column.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 29, 'How does the Commonwealth model gas sales agreement (2025) state the take-or-pay percentage in its definition of Take or Pay Quantity?', '["As 80 percent of the ACQ, the model''s printed default for power sector contracts.", "As [## INSERT] percent of the Adjusted ACQ, left for the parties.", "As 90 percent of the Adjusted ACQ, the figure OIES reports as the market norm.", "As a percentage the regulator fixes each year under PIA s.167(1)."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The definition reads: "quantity of Gas equal to [## INSERT] percent (##%) of the Adjusted Annual Contract Quantity for that Contract Year." The model leaves the figure blank, and the engine takes topPct as an input with no default. The 80 and 90 percent of the Ekene fixtures are those synthetic agreements'' own terms, and PIA s.167(1) concerns the domestic base price.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 30, 'What does the model agreement''s definition of BADQ, the buyer''s annual deficiency quantity, open with?', '["A day on which the buyer takes less than the DCQ it nominated.", "A year in which the seller fails to make the ACQ available.", "Any shortfall at the delivery point, whichever side caused it.", "A contract year in which the buyer does not take at least the take-or-pay quantity."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The definition opens: "means for any Contract Year in which Buyer does not take at least the Take or Pay Quantity,". It is annual, measured against the take-or-pay quantity, and it is the buyer''s. A day''s gap is a buyer shortfall carried to the year''s end, and the seller''s failure is a seller shortfall, which reduces the Adjusted ACQ.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 31, 'What is the permitted reduction in the engine''s Adjusted ACQ rule?', '["A contract''s own further reduction, such as an operational flexibility credit, stated for every year, 0 if none.", "The share of the ACQ the buyer may leave untaken each year before any deficiency arises.", "The seller shortfall damages, deducted from the ACQ at the stated shortfall price.", "A reduction the engine applies by default when the ACQ falls in a leap year."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The rule: Adjusted ACQ = ACQ - maintenance - force majeure - seller shortfall - permitted reduction. The model agreement''s definition ends "and if applicable less the Operational Flexibility Credit"; the engine takes the permitted reduction as a required input for every contract year, 0 when the contract permits none, and refuses a year that leaves it out. The untaken share is what the take-or-pay percentage governs, damages are money, and no reduction is applied by default.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 32, 'Two figures near the take-or-pay edge print the same at six decimals. What decides whether the take-or-pay quantity was met?', '["Their printed agreement, read as equality.", "A comparison of the two figures by eye, rounded to four decimals first.", "The deficiency the engine returns on the exact numbers.", "The reason line''s round-trip figures."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine compares exact numbers. Two figures that print alike at six decimals are not, for that reason alone, met or equal: printed alike is not equal. Only the engine''s own deficiency says whether the take-or-pay quantity was met, and the course keys a figure as equal only where it checks the engine''s two values equal exactly.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 33, 'Why does the engine refuse a take-or-pay year that states no makeUp object, even when only one year is run?', '["A single contract year always opens make-up, which the engine cannot price without terms.", "It must know what happens to a deficiency, and the make-up terms carry no default.", "Its make-up price is required to compute the deficiency payment of the year in every case.", "A one-year run is refused outright; the engine reconciles two years at the least."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine''s own words: "makeUp must be an object { periodYears, order, endOfTerm } (no default); got nothing". The make-up period, the recovery order and the end-of-term rule are contract terms the engine will not guess. A one-year case runs once they are stated, and the deficiency payment uses the take-or-pay price.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 34, 'The power plant states take-or-pay at 80 percent and the export feed at 90 percent. What does the course say the higher percentage expresses?', '["More room for the buyer to take less gas in a poor year without paying for it.", "A larger MaxDCQ, the two moving together.", "A lower effective swing for the buyer, which the Act requires of export sales.", "Steadier revenue the seller can finance a development on."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A high take-or-pay percentage, such as the export feed''s 90, gives the seller a steady revenue it can finance on; a lower one, such as the power plant''s 80, gives the buyer more room in a bad year. The percentage is negotiated alongside swing, and effective swing combines the two; no Act sets it.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 35, 'Two views print two take-or-pay figures for the plant''s 2027. Why does the one-year reconciliation owe less than the planning figure?', '["Force majeure and the seller shortfall come off the ACQ before the percentage applies.", "The contract quantities view uses 90 percent and the take-or-pay view uses 80.", "Over-take of the year, deducted from the quantity owed.", "The take-or-pay view counts 2027 as a leap year of 366 days, and the other does not."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The contract quantities view applies 80 percent to the full ACQ, 6132000.000000, before any adjustment. The take-or-pay year first takes off the force majeure of 42000 and the seller shortfall of 6300, reaching an Adjusted ACQ of 7616700.000000, and 80 percent of that is 6093360.000000. Both views use the same percentage and the same 365-day year.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 36, 'In a single contract year at this tier, which terms of the engine''s money sum are zero, and why?', '["The deficiency payment and the damages, which the engine settles only in the year after.", "The counted gas and the contract price, which one year leaves to the take-or-pay price.", "Make-up taken and the refund: make-up belongs to a later year and a refund to the end of a longer term.", "None: every term is live in each year, including any refund due at the end of the term."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Money in a contract year is counted x contract price + make-up taken x make-up price + deficiency payment - seller shortfall x shortfall price - refund. In one year no make-up is taken and no refund arises, which leaves the counted gas at the contract price, the deficiency payment and any seller shortfall damages.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 37, 'What does a deficiency payment the engine computes NOT say?', '["Which take-or-pay percentage and price produced it, since the engine prints neither of the two.", "What a buyer will actually pay; it is what the stated clauses produce on stated takes.", "Whether the year had a deficiency at all, which the reason line alone reports.", "How much gas was counted, which the one take-or-pay year view leaves unprinted."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A computed figure is no forecast: a deficiency payment is what the stated clauses produce on stated takes. The engine prints the percentage, the price in its reason, the deficiency and the counted gas, and the course quotes each figure with the terms it depends on.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 38, 'The single-year golden case pays 1200.000000. What must be quoted beside that figure?', '["The domestic base price, as reported.", "The make-up period, since the payment is refunded when the make-up period ends.", "Nothing: a deficiency payment is a fixed sum under Article 12.6 of the model.", "Its take-or-pay percentage of 90 and its take-or-pay price."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A figure that depends on a term is quoted with it: a deficiency payment with its take-or-pay percentage and price. Change either and the payment changes. The golden case prices at its own stated take-or-pay price, the model agreement leaves both terms to the parties, and nothing in one year refunds a payment.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 39, 'Which of these can an Associate capstone value never depend on?', '["The domestic base price.", "The day count the capstone card states for its contract year and the calendar it reads.", "The heating value basis and the reference conditions stated on the card for the volume.", "The take-or-pay price stated for the contract year, apart from its contract price."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Each graded value is a return value of the engine on the card''s stated terms, and none uses the domestic base price, which is quoted only as reported; each is also the same number under every reading the engine states. The day count, the heating value basis and the take-or-pay price are stated terms every graded figure may rest on.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 40, 'What is the mirror of take-or-pay in a gas sales agreement?', '["Make-up, which the seller owes when it cannot deliver in the year.", "Over-take, which the buyer pays at the contract price on busy days.", "Damages the seller pays for properly nominated gas it did not make available.", "The Domestic Gas Delivery Obligation penalty, paid by the buyer."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Take-or-pay protects the seller against a buyer that does not take; the mirror clause protects the buyer against a seller that does not deliver, with damages per unit at a stated rate. Make-up is gas the buyer paid for and takes later, over-take is ordinary gas taken above the adjusted DCQ, and the DGDO penalty falls on a lessee under the Act.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 41, 'In the one take-or-pay year view, what is regular revenue?', '["The deficiency payment plus the counted gas, at the take-or-pay price of the year.", "The net to the seller before any refund at the end of the delivery period is paid.", "The take-or-pay quantity at the contract price, whatever the buyer actually took.", "The counted gas at the contract price."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The panel prints each year''s regular revenue, the counted gas at the contract price, beside the deficiency payment, the shortfall damages and the net to the seller. Regular revenue plus the deficiency payment, less the shortfall damages, gives the net in a single year with no make-up or refund.'),
  ('gsa', 'beginner', 'final', null, 42, 'The quantity calculator''s reasons print figures such as 1.5 and 7875. How should a learner use them?', '["Quote them directly, because a reason carries the exact double the engine holds.", "To understand a result, quoting the fields at six decimals when stating it.", "As estimates to be checked against the tiles, which the panel rounds for display.", "As refusals whenever they appear, since a reason means the engine stopped."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A figure inside a reason is the shortest decimal that reads back to the value the engine holds, so it can carry many digits or few. The course quotes the numeric fields at six decimals, and a reason may appear only verbatim as the engine''s own words. A reason sits beside a result; a refusal returns no figures.');
